(function () {
  var SSC_JE_PYG = { esemain: [] };
  function P(q, a, h, s) { return { q: q, a: a, h: h || "", s: s || "" }; }
  function E(exam, q, a, h, s) { var x = P(q, a, h, s); x.exam = exam; return x; }
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '19 | TOM", "What is a kinematic pair? How are kinematic pairs classified? Explain.", "A kinematic pair is the assembly of two links joined together such that their relative motion is completely constrained. Classification: (a) nature of contact - lower pair (surface contact) and higher pair (point/line contact); (b) nature of mechanical constraint - closed pair (links held together mechanically) and unclosed/forced-closed pair (kept in contact by external force, e.g. cam and follower under spring force); (c) nature of relative motion - sliding, turning (revolute), rolling, screw (helical) and spherical pairs.", "Relative motion of the two elements is completely constrained", "Lower-pair examples: piston-cylinder (sliding), shaft in bearing (turning), nut on screw (screw). Higher-pair examples: gear teeth, cam-follower, ball/roller bearing, a rolling wheel on a rail. In a kinematic chain every link forms two pairs; making one link fixed converts the chain into a mechanism. DOF is found with Gruebler's criterion n = 3(l - 1) - 2j - h."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '19 | Machining", "In a machining operation a tolerance of +/- 0.5 mm is kept. Explain the meaning of unilateral and bilateral tolerances.", "Tolerance is the total permissible variation in the size of a part. A bilateral tolerance allows deviation on both sides of the nominal size (e.g. 50 +/- 0.5 mm, the actual size may lie anywhere between 49.5 and 50.5 mm). A unilateral tolerance allows deviation on only one side of the basic size (e.g. 50+0.5/0 or 50+0.0/-0.5).", "Both signs with one number - bilateral; deviation on one side only - unilateral", "In production drawings shafts and holes are normally given unilateral tolerances using the basic-hole or basic-shaft system so that the maximum material condition rests on the safe side. The given +/- 0.5 mm is a bilateral tolerance. ESE expects each term to be explained with an example dimension and a note on which type is preferred for interchangeable manufacture."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '19 | Machine Design", "A hollow shaft for a marine engine is to be designed for a power of 5 MW at 100 r.p.m. Point out how the two sides of the problem will be handled.", "Two sides: strength and rigidity (stiffness). Strength side: the torque is found from power, T = (P x 60)/(2*pi*N) = (5 x 10^6 x 60)/(2*pi*100) = 4.775 x 10^5 N.m, and the hollow section is sized against the induced shear stress tau = T/Zp where Zp = (pi/16) Do^3(1 - a^4), a = Di/Do, using a safe allowable shear stress. Rigidity side: limit the angle of twist with theta = T.L/(G.J) <= theta-allow where J for a hollow shaft = (pi/32) Do^4(1 - a^4). The larger of the two diameters governs; wall thickness t = Do(1 - a)/2.", "Strength: tau <= tau-allow with tau = T/Zp; Rigidity: theta = T.L/(G.J) <= theta-allow", "Choose a = Di/Do in the range 0.5-0.6 for hollow shafts. Compute Do from the strength equation and separately Do from the torsion (rigidity) equation for the shaft length, then select the larger. Marine shafts are usually long, so the rigidity check frequently governs."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '19 | Machine Design", "For a 3-armed crane, the load transmitted to the mast is wx, whereas the mast is subjected to an axial force P and a bending moment M. Describe how the maximum and minimum stresses will be evaluated (direct stress combined with bending).", "Superpose the direct and bending stresses. Direct axial stress sigma-a = P/A is uniform over the section; the bending stress is sigma-b = M/Z, tension on one face and compression on the other. Result: sigma-max = P/A + M/Z (where the two add) and sigma-min = P/A - M/Z (where they oppose). Safety requires both to lie within the permissible stress range.", "sigma-max = P/A + M/Z; sigma-min = P/A - M/Z", "sigma-b is evaluated at the extreme fibre with Z = I/y. If P/A > M/Z the whole section stays in tension; if P/A < M/Z part of the section reverses sign and a stress neutral axis exists. Combined direct-plus-bending is the standard ESE treatment for crane masts, columns and eccentrically loaded bases."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '19 | SOM", "Define coefficient of fluctuation of speed and coefficient of steadiness of a flywheel.", "Coefficient of fluctuation of speed Cs = (omega-max - omega-min)/omega-mean = (Nmax - Nmin)/Nmean. Coefficient of steadiness = 1/Cs = omega-mean/(omega-max - omega-min). A small Cs means a steady speed.", "Cs = (omega-max - omega-min)/omega-mean; steadiness = reciprocal of Cs", "The fluctuation of energy dE stored in the flywheel is related to Cs by dE = I.omega-mean^2.Cs, from which the required moment of inertia I and hence the rim dimensions are found. State the definitions both with angular velocities and with r.p.m. as ESE asks."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '19 | Machining", "A steel shaft is supported on two bearings and machined on a lathe between centers with the usual dead and live centers. How is the shaft aligned and supported during turning?", "The work is mounted between the headstock center and the tailstock dead center; the axis of rotation is the line joining the two centers, so the centers define the alignment. A carrier (lathe dog) fixed to the work engages a pin on the faceplate so the headstock drives the rotation. Alignment of the tailstock to the headstock center is set by shifting the tailstock barrel so both center points lie on the lathe axis.", "Between centers on the axis joining the two centers; driven by a carrier (dog)", "Long slender shafts need steady rests or follower rests to prevent deflection under cutting forces. Alignment is verified by turning a test bar and checking with a dial indicator, or by the two-center alignment setup, so the turned diameter is uniform along the length."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '19 | Machine Design", "An axially loaded rod has its minimum cross-sectional area limited by stress. Determine the shaft diameter under three failure theories for a bending moment M = 7.5 kN.m and twisting moment T = 10 kN.m with allowable stress 160 MPa, and give the probable reason for the difference in diameters.", "Section modulus A = pi.d^3/32, sigma = 32M/(pi d^3), tau = 16T/(pi d^3). Principal stresses: sigma-1 = sigma/2 + sqrt(sigma^2/4 + tau^2), sigma-2 = sigma/2 - sqrt(sigma^2/4 + tau^2). (i) Max shear stress theory (Guest): tau-max = sqrt(sigma^2/4 + tau^2) <= sigma-allow/2, giving d = 86.0 mm. (ii) Max strain energy theory (Haigh, nu = 0.24): sigma-1^2 + sigma-2^2 - 2.nu.sigma-1.sigma-2 = sigma-allow^2 gives d = 88.5 mm. (iii) Max shear strain energy theory (von Mises-Hencky): sigma-1^2 - sigma-1.sigma-2 + sigma-2^2 = sigma-allow^2 gives d = 90.0 mm. The diameters differ because each theory uses a different equivalent-stress criterion; the energy theories give a slightly larger (safer) shaft than the classical maximum-shear criterion.", "MSST: (16/pi d^3) sqrt(M^2 + T^2) <= sigma-allow/2; Haigh and Mises use the principal-stress equations given", "Quick path only if asked: M = 7.5, T = 10 kN.m, sigma-allow = 160 MPa. (i) Meq = sqrt(M^2 + T^2) = 12.5 kN.m -> sigma = 32 Meq/(pi d^3) <= 160 MPa -> d = 86.0 mm. (ii) sigma-1 = 320000/A, sigma-2 = -80000/A with A = pi.d^3/32; strain-energy equation with nu = 0.24 -> d = 88.5 mm. (iii) Mises equation -> d = 90.0 mm. Show principal-stress derivation and the reason for differing diameters for full marks."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '19 | FM", "A jet of water issues horizontally from a fireman's nozzle of diameter 25 mm at a height of 28 m above the ground and strikes the ground 12 m in front of the nozzle. Estimate the rate of discharge.", "Area A = (pi/4)(0.025)^2 = 4.909 x 10^-4 m^2. For a horizontal jet the vertical fall is independent of the jet speed: y = (1/2) g t^2 gives t = sqrt(2y/g) = sqrt(56/9.81) = 2.389 s; the horizontal reach gives V = x/t = 12/2.389 = 5.02 m/s. Discharge Q = A.V = 4.909 x 10^-4 x 5.02 = 2.47 x 10^-3 m^3/s = 2.47 L/s.", "x = V.t; y = (1/2) g t^2; Q = A.V", "t = sqrt(2 x 28/9.81) = 2.389 s, V = 12/2.389 = 5.02 m/s, Q = (pi/4)(0.025)^2 x 5.02 = 2.47 x 10^-3 m^3/s. Note: in the textbook hose-stream problem a vertical-jet or inclined-jet variant is often set; quote this self-consistent solution and state your assumption."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '19 | FM", "A two-dimensional flat plate of width 0.3 m is towed at 2 m/s in water (nu = 1.02 x 10^-6 m^2/s). For the laminar boundary layer: (i) estimate the boundary layer thickness at 10 cm from the leading edge, (ii) indicate its slope at that point, (iii) estimate the drag.", "(i) Blasius laminar thickness delta = sqrt(60.nu.x/U); at x = 0.1 m, delta = sqrt(60 x 1.02 x 10^-6 x 0.1/2) = 1.75 mm. (ii) Slope d(delta)/dx = delta/(2x) = 1.75 x 10^-3/0.2 = 8.75 x 10^-3. (iii) Momentum thickness at the trailing edge x = 0.3 m, theta = delta/15 = 2.02 x 10^-4 m; mean skin-friction coefficient CD = 2.theta/L = 1.35 x 10^-3; drag D = CD.(1/2).rho.U^2.A = 1.35 x 10^-3 x 0.5 x 1000 x 4 x (0.3 x 1) = 0.81 N.", "delta = sqrt(60.nu.x/U); slope = delta/(2x); CD = 2.theta/L", "delta grows as x^(1/2) and the local skin friction decays along the plate. For comparison the turbulent value is delta = 0.37 x/Re^0.2, which ESE sometimes contrasts with the laminar result. Use rho = 1000 kg/m^3 for water."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '22 | Engineering Mechanics", "The rotor of a turbojet engine has a mass of 250 kg and radius of gyration 25 cm; it rotates at 12000 r.p.m. clockwise viewed from the front. The aircraft, flying at 1500 km/hr, turns to the right on a radius of 2 km. Compute the gyroscopic moment exerted on the structure and state whether the nose tends to rise or fall.", "I = m.k^2 = 250 x 0.25^2 = 15.625 kg.m^2; omega = 2.pi x 12000/60 = 1256.6 rad/s; precession omega-p = V/R = (1500/3.6)/2000 = 0.2083 rad/s. Gyroscopic couple C = I.omega.omega-p = 15.625 x 1256.6 x 0.2083 = 4.09 x 10^3 N.m = 4.09 kN.m. The nose tends to RISE.", "C = I.omega.omega-p = 4.09 kN.m; nose rises", "Sense rule: rotor spins clockwise viewed from the front, so its angular-momentum vector points forward along the flight axis; turning right sweeps the momentum vector downward as seen from the rear, and the reaction couple applied to the structure pitches the nose upward (classic result: clockwise-from-front rotor, right turn -> nose rises)."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '22 | TOM", "What is a planetary (epicyclic) gear train? State its main uses. In the train shown, sun gear C is fixed, gear B is on the input shaft at 1000 r.p.m. counter-clockwise seen from the right, and gear F is on the output shaft; the arm A carrying compound planets D-E rotates freely on the output shaft. Using the tabular method, outline how the output speed is obtained.", "An epicyclic gear train is one in which the axes of some gears (the planets) are not fixed - they rotate about axes that themselves move on a rotating arm/carrier. Uses: automobile differentials, planetary gearboxes, hoists/wire-rope drives, watch trains and machine-tool drives - all giving large speed ratios in a compact space. Tabular method: (1) lock the arm and give a unit relative rotation; fill the speed of every gear relative to the arm from the tooth ratios; (2) add the arm's actual speed to each entry; (3) set the fixed sun (C) to zero absolute speed; this is the datum that lets the single unknown (output F speed) be solved.", "Row = gear; column entries = arm-locked speeds + arm speed; then enforce the fixed-gear condition", "Tooth-count data is needed to evaluate the train value e0 (= product of driver teeth / product of driven teeth). With C fixed the equation reduces to n-F = n-A(1 + e0); substitute n-B, the ratios and the signs (a negative result means reversal). ESE gives full credit for the table outline plus the statement that the numeric speed follows from the tooth counts."));


  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '25 | SOM", "A steel tube (E = 200 GPa) with a 32 mm outer diameter and a 4 mm wall thickness is placed in a vice whose jaws just touch the tube ends without force. Forces of 30 kN and 42 kN are then applied to the tube sections (portions AB, BC, CD are each 80 mm long). The vice is then adjusted to decrease the distance between its jaws by 0.2 mm. Determine (i) the forces exerted by the vice on the tube at A and D, and (ii) the change in length of portion BC.", "(i) Compatibility: sum of the segmental changes delta = P.L/(A.E) equals the imposed -0.2 mm, and statics gives F_A + 30 = 42 + F_D. With A = pi(32^2-24^2)/4 = 352 mm^2, F_A = 76.66 kN and F_D = 64.66 kN. (ii) Load in BC = F_A - 42 = 34.66 kN, so delta_BC = 34.66e3 x 80/(352 x 200e3) = 0.039 mm.", "delta = P.L/(A.E) summed over AB+BC+CD; then statics", "The three 80 mm portions carry different axial loads: AB sees F_A, BC sees F_A - 42, CD sees F_A - 42 + 30. Write delta = P.L/(A.E) for each, add to -0.2 mm (the jaws close), and solve together with force equilibrium to get F_A, F_D. The portion BC then carries 34.66 kN in tension, giving an elongation of 0.039 mm."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '25 | SOM", "The compound beam in the figure is pin connected at B; portion BC carries a uniformly distributed load of 4 kN/m over its 2 m span. Determine the components of reaction at the supports, neglecting the beam weight and thickness.", "Split the beam at the frictionless pin B into two free bodies. For BC (2 m span, w = 4 kN/m): symmetry gives R_B = R_C = w.L/2 = 4 kN each. Then analyse AB with its own loading and apply force equilibrium at the pin to obtain the remaining reactions at A.", "Draw FBDs of AB and BC separately; the pin transfers an equal-and-opposite force", "A pin-connected compound beam is statically determinate once the two members are separated at the pin. Take moments about B for each member to find the far-end reactions, then sum forces; the pin transmits a common internal force between AB and BC. With the figure's dimensions for AB and its loads, the four reaction components follow directly."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '25 | SOM", "A compound cylinder is formed by shrinking one steel tube of 100 mm internal diameter and 25 mm wall thickness on to another steel tube of 100 mm internal diameter and 25 mm wall thickness (interface radius 75 mm, outer radius 100 mm). Both tubes are steel (E = 210 GPa) and the shrinkage allowance based on radius is 0.02 mm. Determine the pressure set up at the junction due to shrinkage.", "Interference on the radius delta = 0.02 mm at r = 75 mm. Lame equation for the same E on both tubes: delta = (p.r/E)[ (r^2 + Ri^2)/(r^2 - Ri^2) + (Ro^2 + r^2)/(Ro^2 - r^2) ]. Inner factor (5625+2500)/(5625-2500) = 2.6; outer factor (10000+5625)/(10000-5625) = 3.571; sum 6.171. p = 0.02 x 210e3/(75 x 6.171) = 9.07 MPa.", "Interference of radii = sum of radial deflections of inner + outer tubes at the junction", "Each tube deflects under the common junction pressure p. The radial displacement relation from Lame (u = p.r/E x factor) is added for the two tubes and set equal to the 0.02 mm radial interference. Substituting Ri = 50 mm, r = 75 mm, Ro = 100 mm and E = 210 GPa yields the junction pressure, approximately 9.1 MPa."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '25 | TOM", "A pair of 20 deg full-depth involute spur gears meshes with the larger gear on 48 teeth and the pinion on 12 teeth, module 10 mm. Determine (i) the reduction in the addendum of the gear required to avoid interference, and (ii) the contact ratio of the modified pair.", "(i) Pitch radii r_p = 60, r_g = 240 mm; base radii 56.38 & 225.53 mm; a_w(m) = sqrt(r_g^2 cos^2 phi + (r_p + r_g)^2 sin^2 phi) - r_g = sqrt(50864 + 10528) - 240 = 7.78 mm. Standard addendum m = 10 mm, so the required reduction = 10 - 7.78 = 2.22 mm. (ii) Path of contact = sqrt(Ra_g^2 - rb_g^2) + sqrt(Ra_p^2 - rb_p^2) - (r_p + r_g) sin phi = 102.6 + 41.5 - 102.6 = 41.5 mm; base pitch = pi.m.cos phi = 29.52 mm; contact ratio = 41.5/29.52 = 1.41.", "Interference limit: gear addendum tip must not pass the pinion base tangent; contact ratio = path length / base pitch", "The limiting addendum makes the gear tip circle cut the line of action exactly at the pinion base-circle tangent, giving a_w(m) = 7.78 mm, so the 10 mm standard addendum must be reduced by 2.22 mm. Computing the modified path of contact and dividing by the base pitch (pi m cos phi) gives the contact ratio, roughly 1.41, which still satisfies continuous-drive (contact ratio > 1)."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '25 | Machine Design", "A rotating solid shaft of diameter d carries bending moment M and torque T without any axial load. Determine the equivalent bending moment Me using Maximum Normal Stress Theory (MNST) and Distortion Energy Theory (DE), and the equivalent twisting moment Te using Maximum Shear Stress Theory (MSST). Neglect fatigue and stress concentration.", "sigma = 32M/(pi.d^3), tau = 16T/(pi.d^3). MNST: Me = [M + sqrt(M^2 + T^2)]/2. DE (von Mises): Me = sqrt(M^2 + 0.75 T^2). MSST: Te = sqrt(M^2 + T^2).", "Principal stresses in terms of M and T, then the equivalent-stress criterion of each theory", "From the motor analogy sigma-1 = 16/(pi d^3)[M + sqrt(M^2+T^2)], sigma-2 = 16/(pi d^3)[M - sqrt(M^2+T^2)]. MNST sets sigma-1 = sigma-allow -> Me = (M + sqrt(M^2+T^2))/2. DE sets sqrt(sigma-1^2 - sigma-1 sigma-2 + sigma-2^2) = sqrt(sigma^2 + 3 tau^2) -> Me = sqrt(M^2 + 0.75 T^2). MSST sets tau-max = sqrt((sigma/2)^2 + tau^2) = 16Te/(pi d^3) -> Te = sqrt(M^2 + T^2)."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '25 | Engineering Mechanics", "A cart A of mass 200 kg is held on a 30 deg incline so that it just touches the undeformed spring of stiffness K = 50 N/mm. If A is released very slowly, how far down the incline must it move to reach the equilibrium configuration? If A is released suddenly, what is its speed when it reaches that slow-release equilibrium position?", "Equilibrium: K.x = m.g.sin 30 => x = 200 x 9.81 x 0.5/50000 = 0.01962 m = 19.62 mm. Sudden release at that position: fall in PE = m.g.x.sin 30 = 19.24 J; spring energy = (1/2)K.x^2 = 9.62 J; remaining kinetic = 9.62 J, so v = sqrt(2 x 9.62/200) = 0.31 m/s.", "Slow: force balance Kx = mg sin(theta). Sudden: energy balance at the same x", "In the slow release the spring deflects quasistatically until K.x balances the downhill component m.g.sin 30 deg, x = 19.62 mm. For sudden release, at this position the potential loss equals spring energy plus kinetic energy, so the speed is found from K.E. = m.g.x.sin 30 - (1/2)K.x^2, giving about 0.31 m/s."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '25 | TOM", "The torque exerted by an engine crank is T = (5000 + 1500 sin 3theta) N.m where theta is the crank angle. The mean engine speed is 300 rpm and the flywheel with the rotating parts has a mass of 450 kg and radius of gyration 500 mm. Determine (i) the power of the engine, (ii) the percentage fluctuation of speed when the resisting torque is constant, and (iii) the percentage fluctuation of speed when the resisting torque is (5000 + 600 sin theta).", "(i) Mean torque = 5000 N.m, omega = 2.pi.300/60 = 31.42 rad/s, P = 5000 x 31.42 = 157.1 kW. (ii) Excess energy for 1500 sin 3theta, E = 2 x 1500/3 = 1000 J; I = 450 x 0.5^2 = 112.5 kg.m^2; Cs = 1000/(112.5 x 31.42^2) = 0.0090, i.e. 0.90%. (iii) For 600 sin theta, E = 2 x 600 = 1200 J, Cs = 1200/111055 = 0.0108, i.e. 1.08%.", "dE = I omega^2 Cs; fluctuation energy = area between driving and resisting torque curves over one excess lobe", "The mean torque sets the power. The greatest excess energy is the area by which the driving torque exceeds the mean or the resisting level; for a sin(n theta) harmonic the fluctuation is 2 x amplitude/n. Substituting in the flywheel formula dE = I.omega^2.Cs gives the coefficient, and its percentage is Cs x 100. Cases (ii) and (iii) differ only by the harmonic and its amplitude."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '25 | Machine Design", "A pair of 20 deg full-depth involute spur gears has pinion 24 teeth, gear 56 teeth, module 3 mm, face width 30 mm, pinion speed 1200 rpm. Both gears are steel with ultimate tensile strength 600 N/mm^2. Using the velocity factor for dynamic load and taking the service factor as 1.5, determine (i) the beam strength and (ii) the rated power the gears can transmit without bending failure for a factor of safety 1.5. Lewis form factor for 24 teeth = 0.337 and the velocity factor Cv = 3/(3 + v) with v in m/s.", "(i) sigma_b = S_ut/3 = 200 N/mm^2 -> beam strength S_b = sigma_b x b x m x Y = 200 x 30 x 3 x 0.337 = 6066 N. (ii) Pitch-line velocity v = pi x 3 x 24 x 1200/60 = 4524 mm/s = 4.52 m/s; Cv = 3/7.52 = 0.399; safe tangential load F_t = Cv.S_b/(service x FoS) = 0.399 x 6066/2.25 = 1076 N; rated power = F_t x v = 1076 x 4.52 = 4.86 kW.", "Lewis beam strength Sb = sigma_b b m Y; then F_t = Cv.Sb/(K_a x n); power = F_t.v", "The pinion is the weaker member (24 teeth, Y = 0.337) so it governs. Allowable tooth stress is taken as one-third of the ultimate strength, giving S_b = 6066 N. The dynamic condition enters through the velocity factor Cv; dividing by the service factor and the factor of safety gives the safe tangential force, and multiplying by the pitch-line velocity yields the rated power of roughly 4.9 kW."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '25 | SOM", "For the beam shown in the figure (reactions R_A = 1 kN and R_B = 4 kN), find the value of the overhang dimension a and draw the bending moment diagram (UDL 2 kN/m over AB, a point load 1 kN at the free end C, AB = 2 m, BC = 1 m).", "Take moments of the loads about the supports to relate the reactions to the geometry, solve for a, then cut the beam to write M(x) over AB and over the overhang. Peak values are the midspan moment of the UDL part and the support moment M_B = 1 kN x 1 m = 1 kN.m.", "M(x) piecewise: AB carries the UDL, the overhang carries the free-end point load", "This is a statically determinate beam once the overhang and loads are fixed; the two reactions are given so a follows from vertical-force and moment balance. The BMD is drawn by computing the span moment from the UDL (parabolic, zero at the supports when the ends are simply supported) and the constant-ratio linear moment from the 1 kN end force on the overhang, joining at support B."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '25 | Vibrations", "A torsional pendulum has a natural frequency of 200 cycles/min vibrating in a vacuum; the disc has a mass moment of inertia of 0.2 kg.m^2. Immersed in oil its natural frequency is found to be 180 cycles/min. Determine the damping constant. If the disc in oil is given an initial angular displacement of 2 deg, find its displacement at the end of the first cycle.", "omega_n = 2.pi.200/60 = 20.94 rad/s; omega_d = 2.pi.180/60 = 18.85 rad/s; zeta = sqrt(1 - (omega_d/omega_n)^2) = sqrt(1 - 0.9^2) = 0.4359. c_c = 2 I omega_n = 2 x 0.2 x 20.94 = 8.38 N.m.s/rad, so c = zeta.c_c = 3.65 N.m.s/rad. Amplitude ratio per damped cycle = exp(-2.pi.zeta/sqrt(1-zeta^2)) = exp(-3.04) = 0.048; displacement after the first cycle = 2 x 0.048 = 0.096 deg.", "omega_d = omega_n sqrt(1-zeta^2); c = zeta(2 I omega_n); ratio per cycle = e^(-2pi zeta/sqrt(1-zeta^2))", "The drop from 200 to 180 cycles/min gives omega_n and omega_d, hence the damping ratio from the damped-frequency relation. Critical damping for the torsion system is c_c = 2 I omega_n, and the actual coefficient is c = zeta.c_c = 3.65 N.m.s/rad. Under damping the successive amplitudes decay by exp(-2.pi.zeta/sqrt(1-zeta^2)) each cycle, so the 2 deg initial displacement is reduced to about 0.1 deg after the first cycle."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '25 | Vibrations", "The natural frequency of vibration of a person is found to be 5.2 Hz while standing on a horizontal floor. Assuming negligible damping, determine (A) the equivalent stiffness of his body in the vertical direction if the mass is 70 kg, and (B) the amplitude of vertical displacement of the person if the floor is subjected to a vertical harmonic vibration of frequency 5.3 Hz and amplitude 0.1 m due to an unbalanced rotating machine on the floor.", "(A) omega_n = 2.pi.5.2 = 32.67 rad/s; k = m.omega_n^2 = 70 x 1067.2 = 74,705 N/m = 74.7 kN/m. (B) omega = 2.pi.5.3 = 33.30 rad/s, r = 33.30/32.67 = 1.019; X/A = 1/|1 - r^2| = 25.7, so X = 25.7 x 0.1 = 2.57 m.", "k = m omega_n^2; undamped transmissibility X/A = 1/|1-r^2| with r = omega/omega_n", "The body is modelled as a single-DOF mass-spring system, so the stiffness follows from the measured natural frequency. Since the operating frequency (5.3 Hz) is only slightly above the natural frequency, the system vibrates just past resonance and the displacement magnification |1/(1-r^2)| is large, amplifying the 0.1 m floor motion to roughly 2.6 m."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '25 | Machine Design", "A welded connection of steel plates (two fillet welds of equal length 50 mm each, throat dimension t) is subjected to an eccentric force of 10 kN with eccentricity e = 175 mm from the weld group centroid. Assuming static conditions, determine the throat dimension if the permissible shear stress is limited to 95 N/mm^2.", "The weld group carries a direct shear tau_d = P/A_t and a secondary torsional shear tau_t = P.e.r_max/J (polar moment of area and centroid computed from the weld dimensions in the figure). Combine vectorially, set the resultant equal to tau_allow = 95 MPa and solve for the throat t (figures of the weld layout are required for r_max and J; with the drawn weld geometry this gives t of the order of 5-6 mm).", "tau_total = sqrt( (P/A_t)^2 + (P.e.r_max/J)^2 ); throat area A_t proportional to t", "An eccentric weld is designed by superposing direct shear (P over the total throat area) with the secondary shear from the applied couple P.e resisted by the weld's polar moment of inertia. The throat dimension scales both A_t and J, so equating the maximum combined shear to 95 N/mm^2 fixes t. The exact figure dimensions fix r_max and the weld-group geometry used in J."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '25 | SOM", "Find the position of the centroid of the T-section whose flange is 100 x 15 mm and web is 135 x 15 mm (total depth 150 mm). A cantilever of length 3000 mm of this section, flange at the top, carries a load W at the free end. What is the maximum value of W if the stress in the section is not to exceed 50 N/mm^2?", "Centroid from the top: y = (1500 x 7.5 + 2025 x 75)/3525 = 46.3 mm, so distances to the extreme fibres are 46.3 mm (top) and 103.7 mm (bottom). I = I_flange + A1 d1^2 + I_web + A2 d2^2 = 7.03 x 10^6 mm^4. For M = 3000 W at the fixed end, sigma = Mc/I <= 50; bottom fibre (tension, c = 103.7 mm) governs: W = 50 x 7.03e6/(3000 x 103.7) = 1.13 kN (approx 1.1-1.3 kN depending on exact rounding).", "y = sum(A.y)/sum(A); I = sum(I_own + A.d^2); sigma = M.c/I in the extreme fibre", "Locate the neutral axis by the composite-area centroid, then parallel-axis theorem gives the second moment about it. The larger of the two extreme-fibre distances (the web-side, 103.7 mm) governs, so W follows from sigma_allow = 3000.W.c/I. The flange rule (top fibre, tension) is the lesser constraint here, so W is set by the bottom fibre stress."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '25 | TOM", "What is the controlling force of a governor? Draw a typical controlling force diagram for spring-controlled governors and explain how it helps in establishing the stability or instability of the governor.", "Controlling force F_c = m.omega^2.r is the centrifugal force the governor mechanism must balance at radius r. In the F_c-r diagram a stable governor has the controlling-force line (solid, e.g. F_c = K.r - constant) cutting the F_c = m omega^2 r lines with dF_c/dr greater than F_c/r; an unstable one has the force line below the origin-slope line, and isochronism when parallel to it.", "Stability: dF_c/dr > F_c/r; isochronism: F_c curve passes through the origin", "For the rotating balls, F_c = m.omega^2.r. A spring-controlled governor has a straight controlling-force line on the F_c-r plot. Its intersection with the centrifugal lines at each speed gives the operating radius. If the controlling force line becomes flatter than the origin-line (dF_c/dr < F_c/r) a small disturbance moves the balls to a new equilibrium of wrong sense - the governor is unstable. Equality gives isochronous behaviour."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '25 | TOM", "In a Porter governor each arm is 250 mm long and the arms are pivoted on the governor axis. Weight of sleeve = 300 N, weight of each ball = 50 N. The radii of rotation of the balls corresponding to the minimum and maximum speeds are 130 mm and 170 mm. Determine the range of speed, neglecting friction. If sleeve friction is equivalent to 25 N of load at the sleeve, determine how the speed range is modified.", "For arms pivoted on the axis: N^2 = (895/h)[(W + 2w)/(2w)]. h_min = sqrt(250^2 - 130^2) = 213.5 mm, N_min = sqrt(895/0.2135 x 4) = 129.5 rpm; h_max = sqrt(250^2 - 170^2) = 183.3 mm, N_max = sqrt(895/0.1833 x 4) = 139.8 rpm. Range 129.5-139.8 (10.3 rpm). With 25 N sleeve friction the effective sleeve load is W - 25 at min speed and W + 25 at max speed: N_min = sqrt(895/0.2135 x 3.25) = 116.7 rpm, N_max = sqrt(895/0.1833 x 4.25) = 144.1 rpm - the range widens to 116.7-144.1 rpm.", "Porter with pivots on the axis: N^2 = (895/h)(W + 2w)/(2w); friction widens the range", "The sleeve height h = sqrt(L^2 - r^2) at each extreme radius gives the corresponding speed through the Porter formula. Neglecting friction the range is about 10 rpm. Friction opposes sleeve motion, so at the minimum radius the sleeve is effectively lighter (W - 25) and at the maximum effectively heavier (W + 25); this widens the speed range to roughly 116.7-144.1 rpm."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '25 | Machine Design", "The horizontal shaft ABCD is mounted in bearings at B and D. A belt passes over the 250 mm diameter pulley fixed at A (tight and slack tensions in the ratio 4:1, horizontal strands) and a gear pinion of 150 mm pitch diameter is mounted at C, its tangential force vertical. Ascertain the safety factor of the shaft when transmitting 20 kW from the belt to the pinion at a steady 450 rpm, taking the yield strength of the ductile material as 500 MPa. Neglect fatigue and stress concentration; use Maximum Shear Stress Theory. (The axial positions and shaft diameters are given in the figure.)", "Torque T = 60P/(2.pi.N) = 60 x 20000/(2.pi x 450) = 127.3 N.m. Belt: (F1 - F2) x 0.125 = T -> F1 - F2 = 1018.6 N with F1 = 4F2 -> F2 = 339.5 N, F1 = 1358.1 N; pinion tangential force = T/0.075 = 1698 N. From the figure's spans compute the bearing reactions and the bending moment diagram; then sigma = 32M/(pi d^3), tau = 16T/(pi d^3) and the MSST safety factor = S_yt/(sqrt(sigma^2 + 4 tau^2)) at the most stressed section.", "Combine bending from belt and pinion loads with torsion; MSST: S_yt/sqrt(sigma^2+4tau^2)", "The power and speed fix the torque, and from it the two belt tensions (4:1) and the pinion tangential force. Vertical and horizontal force-balance over the given span (figure) give the bending moment at each section, with the most critical section where M is a maximum. The equivalent stress by MSST is sqrt(sigma^2 + 4 tau^2), and the factor of safety is the yield strength divided by it."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '25 | IM/OR", "A manufacturing unit has to supply 4200 units of a product per year. Set-up cost per run is Rs 75 and inventory carrying cost is Rs 1.5 per unit per annum; shortages are not permitted. Determine (i) the economic order quantity, (ii) the optimum number of orders per annum, (iii) the minimum average annual inventory cost, and (iv) the optimum period of supply per optimum order.", "(i) EOQ = sqrt(2DS/H) = sqrt(2 x 4200 x 75/1.5) = sqrt(420000) = 648.1 = 648 units. (ii) Orders = D/Q = 4200/648 = 6.5 per year. (iii) Min cost = sqrt(2DSH) = sqrt(2 x 4200 x 75 x 1.5) = Rs 972.1 per year. (iv) Period = Q/D = 648/4200 = 0.1543 year = 56.3 days.", "EOQ = sqrt(2DS/C); at the optimum, ordering cost = carrying cost", "In the classic lot-size model the total annual cost = D.S/Q + (Q/2).H is minimised when the two terms are equal, giving Q* = sqrt(2DS/H). Here D = 4200, S = 75, H = 1.5, giving 648 units per lot, about 6.5 lots per year, a minimum cost of Rs 972/year, and a supply period of just over 56 days."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '25 | Materials", "A round specimen of wrought iron, diameter 12.5 mm and gauge length 100 mm, is tested in tension to fracture. Observations: load at yield 29.5 kN, maximum load 44 kN, load at fracture 37 kN, neck diameter at fracture 9.2 mm, total extension of the specimen 28.5 mm, E = 200 kN/mm^2. Calculate (i) yield strength, (ii) ultimate strength, (iii) actual breaking stress, (iv) percentage elongation, and (v) modulus of resilience at the yield-point stress.", "A0 = pi x 12.5^2/4 = 122.7 mm^2. (i) sigma_y = 29.5e3/122.7 = 240.4 MPa. (ii) sigma_u = 44e3/122.7 = 358.6 MPa. (iii) Breaking on the neck area A_n = pi x 9.2^2/4 = 66.5 mm^2: 37e3/66.5 = 556.4 MPa. (iv) % elongation = 28.5/100 x 100 = 28.5%. (v) Modulus of resilience = sigma_y^2/(2E) = 240.4^2/(2 x 200000) = 0.145 N.mm/mm^3 (MPa).", "Stress on original area except breaking stress uses the necked area; resilience = sigma_y^2/(2E)", "Yield and ultimate strengths are the loads divided by the ORIGINAL area; the true breaking stress uses the reduced necked cross-section, hence its higher value. Percentage elongation and the yield stress drive the resilience, E_store = sigma_y^2/(2E) = 0.145 MPa, the elastic strain-energy density absorbed up to yielding."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '25 | Mechatronics", "Two systems are represented by their system matrices (given in the figure). Determine their characteristic equations and comment on the stability of each.", "For each system find det(sI - A) = 0; apply the Routh-Hurwitz criterion to the characteristic polynomial. With the matrices shown, one system has roots with negative real parts (stable) while the other has a positive real root / positive-real-part complex pair and is therefore unstable.", "Stability is decided by the signs of the real parts of the roots of det(sI - A)", "The system matrix approach sets stability from the eigenvalues of A, which are the roots of the characteristic polynomial. Routh's table or direct root evaluation tells whether any root lies in the right half-plane; a positive real part (or positive real root) makes the system unstable, and all-negative real parts make it stable. The pair of matrices in the ESE problem demonstrate both cases."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '25 | Vibrations", "A machine component performs a harmonic motion and a vibrometer with a natural frequency of 5 rad/s and damping ratio 0.3 is attached to it. If the maximum and minimum recorded values differ by 6 mm, find the amplitude of motion of the vibrating component when its frequency is 30 rad/s (with the ACE solution using omega = 25 rad/s).", "The vibrometer measures the relative displacement, whose amplitude is X_r = X.r^2/sqrt((1-r^2)^2 + (2.zeta.r)^2). For omega = 25 rad/s, r = 5: X_r/X = 25/sqrt(576 + 9) = 1.0336; recorded half-peak X_r = 3 mm, hence X = 3/1.0336 = 2.90 mm. (For omega = 30 rad/s, r = 6, X_r/X = 36/sqrt(1225 + 13) = 1.023, giving X = 2.93 mm.)", "Relative-amplitude transmissibility of a vibrometer; half peak-to-peak = 3 mm", "A vibrometer measures the relative motion between its mass and the casing; its transfer is R = r^2/sqrt((1-r^2)^2 + (2 zeta r)^2). The 6 mm peak-to-peak reading means a 3 mm relative amplitude. Inverting R with the machine frequency (? = 25 or 30 rad/s against omega_n = 5) gives the component's true amplitude, close to 2.9-2.9 mm."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '25 | Mechatronics", "Determine the inverse of the 4x4 homogeneous transformation matrix that represents a rigid rotation R and translation p.", "For a rigid transformation T = [R p; 0 0 0 1], the inverse is T^-1 = [R^T -R^T.p; 0 0 0 1] because R^T = R^-1 for a rotation. Multiply the bottom-right block by R^T and the translation by -R^T.p.", "Inverse of a rigid-body transform: rotation part transposes, then offset = -R^T p", "A homogeneous transform maps a frame by rotating (R) then translating (p). Reversing the motion, the inverse first undoes the rotation via R^-1 = R^T and then applies the negated translation expressed in the rotated frame, -R^T.p, giving the standard closed form for inverse kinematics and frame-chain inversion."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '25 | IM/OR", "In an organisation, manufacture of a component is composed of 7 activities with the optimistic, most likely and pessimistic durations (weeks) tabulated (activity i-j): 1-2: 2,2,8; 1-3: 2,5,8; 1-4: 3,3,9; 2-5: 2,2,2; 3-5: 3,6,15; 4-6: 3,6,9; 5-6: 4,7,16. Draw the network; find the expected duration and variance of each activity, the expected project length, the critical path, and the variance and standard deviation of the project length.", "te = (a + 4m + b)/6; variance = ((b - a)/6)^2. Activities: 1-2: 3.0, 1-3: 5.0, 1-4: 4.0, 2-5: 2.0, 3-5: 7.0, 4-6: 6.0, 5-6: 8.0. Paths: 1-2-5-6 = 13, 1-3-5-6 = 20, 1-4-6 = 10 weeks. Expected project length = 20 weeks on the critical path 1-3-5-6. Variance = 1 + 4 + 4 = 9, so std. deviation = 3 weeks.", "te = (a+4m+b)/6; critical path = longest chain; add variances of critical activities", "Use the three-estimate averaging for each activity, then the forward/backward pass identifies the longest path. Two paths tie structurally but 1-3-5-6 (20 weeks) clearly dominates. Project variance is the sum of the variances of the critical activities: (8-2)/6 = 1 squared, (15-3)/6 = 2 squared and (16-4)/6 = 2 squared, i.e. 1 + 4 + 4 = 9, so sigma = 3 weeks."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '25 | Production", "In an orthogonal cutting operation with a tool of rake angle 12 deg, the vertical component of the cutting force is 1600 N, the horizontal component is 1250 N, the chip thickness ratio is 0.25 and the cutting speed is 200 m/min. Calculate (i) friction force on the rake face, (ii) normal force on the rake face, (iii) resultant cutting force, (iv) shear force along the shear plane, (v) normal force on the shear plane, (vi) work done in shear, (vii) work done in friction.", "F = Fc.sin a + Ft.cos a = 1600 sin12 + 1250 cos12 = 1555.3 N; N = Fc.cos a - Ft.sin a = 1305.1 N; R = sqrt(Fc^2 + Ft^2) = 2030 N. Shear angle phi = tan^-1 (r.cos a/(1 - r.sin a)) = tan^-1 (0.25 x 0.98/0.948) = 14.5 deg. Fs = Fc cos phi - Ft sin phi = 1237 N; Fn = Fc sin phi + Ft cos phi = 1610 N. Shear velocity Vs = V cos a/cos(phi - a) = 3.26 m/s -> shear work = 1237 x 3.26 = 4.03 kW; chip velocity Vc = r.V = 50 m/min = 0.83 m/s -> friction work = 1555.3 x 0.83 = 1.29 kW.", "F = Fc sin a + Ft cos a; shear angle from r, alpha; Fs = Fc cos phi - Ft sin phi", "Merchant/orthogonal relations resolve the measured power components. The friction and normal components on the rake face follow by rotating R by the rake angle; the shear angle comes from the chip ratio (tan phi = r cos a/(1 - r sin a)); and the shear-plane components from rotating by phi. Multiplying each force by the corresponding velocity (shear velocity Vs, chip velocity r.V) gives the two rates of work."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '25 | Mechatronics", "Generate the forward kinematic model of the 3-DOF RRP (spherical) manipulator arm shown in the figure by (i) drawing and assigning the frames using the DH rules, (ii) writing the DH parameter table, (iii) forming the individual transformations and (iv) the overall transformation matrix.", "Assign bases {0} to {3}: the first two rotations (theta1, theta2) align a reach direction, and the prismatic joint d3 translates along it; coordinate frame conventions (x, z axes and the a, alpha, d, theta parameters) are tabulated for rows 1-3. Each A matrix = Rot(z,theta).Trans(z,d).Trans(x,a).Rot(x,alpha); post-multiplying A1.A2.A3 gives the 4x4 T0-3 = R(p) with the end position p in the spherical-coordinate form.", "T = A1(theta1) x A2(theta2) x A3(d3); DH rows supply a, alpha, d, theta", "The spherical RRP arm sets theta1 as base yaw about the vertical, theta2 as a second rotation defining the pointing direction, and d3 as extension along it - the classical spherical coordinates. Frames are placed per DH convention (z_i along the joint axis, x_i perpendicular to z_{i-1} and z_i), entered into the DH table, and each link transformation A_i is built; their product is the end-effector frame whose translation is the spherical-coordinate position."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '25 | Materials", "Explain the meaning of Miller indices in a unit crystal cell. Determine the Miller indices of the directions A, B and C shown in the figure.", "Miller indices [h k l] of a direction are the smallest set of integers proportional to the components of the direction vector in the crystal coordinate system; indices (h k l) of a plane are the reciprocals of its intercepts on the axes reduced to integers. Read each direction from the figure, express as the vector from the origin, and scale to smallest integers to obtain [h k l] for A, B and C.", "Planes: reciprocals of intercepts; directions: smallest integer ratios of vector components", "The convention quantifies the orientation of crystal planes and directions. For planes, take the reciprocals of the fractional intercepts on a, b, c, clear fractions and enclose in parentheses (h k l). For directions, the vector from the origin is reduced to the smallest integer set and enclosed in square brackets [h k l]; negative components carry an overbar, and instructions (e.g. families <h k l>) may also be requested."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '25 | Production", "A shaper machines a medium-carbon steel workpiece 225 mm long and 125 mm wide, running at 125 cutting strokes per minute with a feed of 0.5 mm per stroke and 5 mm depth of cut. The forward stroke occupies 220 deg of the crank cycle. Calculate the percentage of time when the tool is NOT contacting the workpiece, and the total machining time, taking an approach distance of 30 mm.", "Idle (non-cutting) time fraction = return angle/360 = (360 - 220)/360 = 38.9%. Machining: stroke length = 225 + 30 (approach) = 255 mm; number of strokes = width/feed = 125/0.5 = 250; machining time = strokes / (strokes per min) = 250/125 = 2.0 min (cutting) per the stroke rate.", "Non-contact = return strokes share of the cycle; time = number of strokes / stroke rate", "In a shaper one crank rotation is one cutting stroke plus return. The 220/140 deg split gives 38.9% idle time. The tool only advances during the idle return, so the number of required strokes equals workpiece width divided by feed per stroke (250); dividing by the 125 strokes/min rate gives the total time of about 2 minutes for the pass."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '25 | Production", "A milling operation is done on low-carbon steel with a 125 mm diameter cutter of 10 teeth operating at 25 m/min; table feed rate 120 mm/min and depth of cut 5 mm. Calculate (A) the length of the chip in up and down (climb) milling, and (B) the change in path length from up to down milling.", "Cutter speed N = 1000 x 25/(pi x 125) = 63.7 rpm; feed per tooth fz = 120/(N.z) = 120/637 = 0.19 mm/tooth; arc of contact theta = cos^-1(1 - 2d/D) gives the contact chord, lc = sqrt(D.d) = sqrt(125 x 5) = 25 mm chord. In up milling the chip grows from zero to full thickness (path = 0.5 x feed/rev + lc); in down milling it starts at full depth; the difference in the uncut chip length path is of the order of the feed-related term (figures and convention-dependent).", "Chip length = arc of contact +- feed terms; up vs down differ in entry conditions", "The depth of cut sets the contact arc/chord sqrt(D.d); the feed per tooth and the direction of cutter rotation versus workpiece motion distinguish up (uncut chip builds up) from down (chip starts at maximum) milling. Combining the contact length with the approach term yields the chip path for each; the change between the two is the feed contribution that the ESE problem asks to quantify."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '25 | Mechatronics", "For the given DH parameter table of a manipulator arm, generate the coordinate frames as per the DH rules and give reasons for the selection of the origins and the axes (x, y, z) of each frame from {0} to {3}.", "Frame {0} is fixed at the base with z0 along the joint-1 axis and x0 along the common normal; x1 is placed perpendicular to z0 and z1 (along the common normal between axes 1 and 2); each subsequent origin sits on the intersection of the common normal with its axis. Choices keep a_i = distance along x_i between the z axes, alpha_i the twist about x_i, d_i the offset along z_{i-1} and theta_i the rotation about z_{i-1}, so the table matches the assigned frames.", "z_i along joint axes; x_i along common normals; origins at their intersections", "The DH convention fixes each frame from the pair of neighbouring joint axes: the common perpendicular fixes both x_i direction and the origin position, giving unambiguous a, alpha, d, theta. Annotating why each origin and axis were chosen (base alignment, tool frame, zero of the joint variable) is what the question weights; the table then converts directly into the A matrices."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '25 | Materials", "Explain the laws of corrosion (Faraday's laws of electrolysis) and give examples of two metals following the law in each case. The oxidation loss on a copper surface is 0.05 mm in 15 hours; how much will be the loss in 225 hours?", "Faraday's first law: mass reacted m is proportional to the charge passed (m = Z.I.t); second law: equivalent weights react with the same charge. E.g. copper and zinc in electrodeposition follow m = Z I t; in uniform (atmospheric) oxidation the loss grows with time. Given 0.05 mm/15 h, in 225 h (15x) the uniform-rate loss = 0.05 x 15 = 0.75 mm.", "m proportional to I.t (first law); uniform corrosion rate is linear in time", "Corrosion is electrochemical; Faraday's laws govern the coulombs involved, and uniform corrosion at constant rate converts charge to thickness loss linearly with time. Since 225 h is 15 times 15 h, the linear oxidation loss scales 15-fold to 0.75 mm whenever the corrosion rate is steady (non-passivating, non-localised)."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '25 | Materials", "What are the different 'top-down' and 'bottom-up' methods for the synthesis of nanostructured materials? With a sketch, briefly explain mechanical high-energy ball milling used for the synthesis of nanomaterials.", "Top-down (breaking down bulk): mechanical milling/ball milling, lithography, etching, laser ablation. Bottom-up (building from atoms): sol-gel, chemical vapour deposition, self-assembly, precipitation. High-energy ball milling comminutes bulk powder with hardened steel/zirconia balls in a rotating/vibrating mill; repeated impact and cold welding refine the crystallite size into the nano range.", "Top-down subtractive from bulk; bottom-up additive from atoms/molecules; ball milling is a top-down method", "The two synthesis families differ in the direction of size reduction. Mechanical alloying / high-energy ball milling is the paradigm mechanical top-down route: powder trapped between colliding balls is repeatedly deformed, fractured and cold-welded, refining grain size and sometimes alloying; milling time, ball-to-powder ratio and atmosphere control the final nanoscale crystallite size."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '25 | Production", "Calculate the power required to draw hot-drawn steel wire from 15 mm to 12.5 mm diameter at 120 m/min. The coefficient of friction between the die and the wire is 0.15 and the die (semi) angle is 5 deg. The average flow stress of hot-drawn steel is 30 kgf/mm^2; back pull = 0. Also calculate the maximum reduction possible.", "A0 = pi/4 x 15^2 = 176.7 mm^2, A1 = pi/4 x 12.5^2 = 122.7 mm^2; draw stress (back pull = 0): sigma_d = sigma_f [1 + mu.cot(a)] ln(A0/A1) with sigma_f = 30 x 9.81 = 294 N/mm^2. With semi-angle a = 5 deg, mu.cot 5 = 1.71: sigma_d = 294 x 2.71 x ln(1.44) = 291 N/mm^2. Drawing force = sigma_d x A1 = 35.7 kN; power = F x V = 35.7 x 2 = 71.4 kW. Maximum reduction per pass: ln(A0/A1) <= 1/(1 + mu.cot a) = 0.368 -> A0/A1 <= 1.445, i.e. max reduction 30.8%.", "sigma_d = sigma_f(1 + mu.cot a) ln(A0/A1); power = F.V; max reduction from sigma_d = sigma_f", "Wire drawing without back pull: the drawing stress rises with the reduction (ln area ratio) and friction (the 1 + mu cot a factor). With a 5 deg semi-angle the single-pass reduction 15->12.5 mm (ln 1.44 = 36.5%) is essentially at the attainable limit (about 30-36%), and the draw force of roughly 36 kN at 120 m/min corresponds to a drawing power of around 70 kW."));
  SSC_JE_PYG.esemain.push(E("ESE Mains '25 | SOM", "A 3-element rectangular (0/45/90) rosette is used on a steel machine part. Measured strains: eps0 = -220 micron/m, eps45 = 120 micron/m and eps90 = 220 micron/m (the ACE solution uses eps45 = -120 micron/m). Determine the principal strains and principal stresses using the analytical expressions, with E = 200 GPa and nu = 0.3.", "eps_x = eps0 = -220 mu, eps_y = eps90 = 220 mu, gamma_xy = 2.eps45 - eps_x - eps_y = 2(120) + 220 - 220 = 240 mu (with eps45 = -120, gamma_xy = -240 mu; magnitude identical). Principal strains: eps1,2 = (eps_x+eps_y)/2 +/- sqrt(((eps_x-eps_y)/2)^2 + (gamma_xy/2)^2) = 0 +/- sqrt(220^2 + 120^2) = +/- 251 mu. Principal stresses: sigma1 = E/(1-nu^2)(eps1 + nu.eps2) = 219780 x (1-0.3) x 251 = 38.6 MPa, sigma2 = -38.6 MPa.", "Rectangular rosette: gamma_xy = 2 eps45 - eps_x - eps_y; Hooke's law in 2-D for principal stresses", "For a 0/45/90 rosette the shear strain follows from gamma_xy = 2 eps45 - (eps0 + eps90); here it is +/-240 micron/m. The principal strains follow from the Mohr-circle relations and are opposite and equal at +/-251 micron/m because eps_x and eps_y are balanced. Two-dimensional Hooke's law (dividing by 1 - nu^2) converts these to principal stresses of +/-38.6 MPa. (The printed sign of eps45 only flips the shear-direction convention.)"));

  if (typeof window !== 'undefined') {
    window.SSC_JE_PYG = SSC_JE_PYG;
  }
  if (typeof module !== 'undefined') {
    module.exports = SSC_JE_PYG;
  }
})();
